{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793195363,"nonce":"yqrE6T9lyUrxbfCjKTdkcyBdXVU=","title":"Cache lowered and optimised AIR across compiler invocations","message":"RayDE browser app previews repeatedly invoke ash --build on HashLink bytecode. WASM compilation is slower than the Haxe bytecode stage, so re-lowering and re-optimising unchanged functions is visible during normal preview rebuilds. Add a persistent, bounded AIR cache that can reuse validated lowering/optimisation results across invocations, including edited programs where only some functions changed.\n\nCache identity must include bytecode and relevant type/global/native dependency changes, compiler/AIR schema revision, target triple, ABI/runtime contract, optimisation options and profile inputs. Function-local reuse needs dependency invalidation; do not assume unchanged function bytes imply unchanged semantics. Include native and WASM AOT consumers where valid.\n\nUse atomic publication, validate entries, handle concurrent writers and corrupted/old entries as cache misses, and provide inspection/clear/disable controls with a disk budget/eviction policy. Avoid retaining all AIR in process memory: the existing optimised AIR retention issue is a separate concern. A crash or cancellation must not publish partial entries.\n\nMeasure cold/warm unchanged builds and one-function edits with an Ashui application. Report time in decode/lowering, AIR passes, backend code generation and linking, plus cache hit counts and memory. Verify cached and uncached output behaviour across target/options/schema changes, source edits, corruption and concurrent builds. RayDE can independently cache final bytecode and WASM artifacts; this request concerns reusable compiler work for builds that genuinely need recompilation.","files":null}]}